---
title: Unaddressed Childhood Bereavement Makes Adult Choices
type: grief
summary: A look into how childhood bereavement influences our future
date: 2024-05-01
duration: 10
image: >-
  https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1590982257067-6ab55fd8fe2f?q=80&w=764&auto=format&fit=crop&ixlib=rb-4.1.0&ixid=M3wxMjA3fDB8MHxwaG90by1wYWdlfHx8fGVufDB8fHx8fA%3D%3D
---
The week before Father's Day, a close friend texted me about a boy in her son's class whose father died when he was young. Suddenly, grief pinned me to the bed until I had no option but to pen this.

I am 17, second year at the University of Johannesburg, two years after my mother's death and six after my dad's. I am doing the most flirting I have ever done with the idea of no longer being alive and having to feel. I type, over many days, a lengthy suicide note whose essence is that I feel life is random and there is no grand plan; parents die and leave their children alone and confused. Suicide is never a solution, and I am grateful to be here today to write this, albeit reluctantly, as I do anything that may be seen to promote or risk triggering suicidal thoughts and behaviors. Still, I share this with the hope that reading about my experience may help a parent or guardian grasp the gravity of the often-ignored childhood bereavement, or that someone who needs it will understand that what they are feeling is not uncommon and is beatable. It is beatable, and there is joy to be felt again.

Another crystal clear moment: I'm in what's considered deep trouble for the first time in my school career (about 10 years in), and everyone who was involved has apologized and cried "peer pressure." The principal asks me if I was also under peer pressure, giving me a clear out. I say no. I participated in the protest because I wanted to (I didn't know or say that actually I could have cared less about that student I didn't even know whose expulsion was being protested, but I needed a distraction from my new grief, something else to feel). Principal is in disbelief, disgusted by my disregard for his authority. I'm sneering at him too, calling out the numbers of my grandmother's landline after he threatens to "call your parents." I am drunk with anger, not able to imagine why he would think that in a world where parents die—where my mom had stopped breathing a few weeks earlier while *Holding the Baby* played on TV as if my life wasn't upending unthinkably—why he would dare to begin to imagine I was afraid of a phone call. I had never met the girl I was that day, but she was me.

The wounds of my childhood bereavement almost physically reopen as I type. I was wounded not because I didn't have a well-intentioned guardian in my grandmother. Rather, humongous feelings were left unaddressed as feelings on their own, outside of what and who I had (a big note for those supporting grievers: it is not personal, you are just not the dead person). My big feelings did have an outlet in my endless scribing and processing on the page, a practice about which my first guest, poet Rethabile Masilo, [speaks positively](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=m91bC8HrwfE&list=PLfs2NHMZSv4oD5vwScw6Fjn8kqadds2G1&index=2). I know I needed, and continue to need, more. Therapy is a central component of that. Ultimately, however, for me it was the beckoning of the blank page that became one of the saving graces.

Why did none of the adults around me at the time appreciate and address me at the level of my very new bereavement, mourning, grief? In our survival-centric society, there is a permeating and damaging belief that we cannot dwell on "these things," lest we breed lazy, rebellious children who use their grief as an excuse, a never-ending ticket to misbehave, and end up needing more management than we have the time to give. We respond to bereaved children out of our fear, pain, our (sometimes) physical and in many instances, mental poverty. So we let grief rule without knowing it. We let the grief do the managing, letting it take over and wreak varying levels of havoc.

The havoc is not always so apparent. Over the years, the grief stitches itself so seamlessly into your psyche that you do not recognize it as what it is. I never recognized my anger as grief at all. Or my self-sacrificing loyalty. Even years later, I didn’t always register my helicopter parenting as a byproduct of my childhood grief, or my at-times frantic mom moment-creating. Sometimes I stop and think, this is a moment I hope my children remember me by, as though I were dying tomorrow. There are also more obvious effects: the prayers, the ceaseless prayers that God grants me life until my children are at least done with college―the bar so low, so desperate to live that I can't risk asking for too much.

This is some of what it has meant for me to live with cumulative grief, something Episode 6 guest [Mufaro discusses](https://youtu.be/9udBl_OkbcY?si=GhDDu_2SoG-MxtFc). As I get to the end of Season 1 of my podcast, Granules of Grief with Refiloe, I hope to speak to adults who were bereaved in childhood, who've looked into what it means to grieve when you don't yet have a concept of grief, utterly broken when you cannot begin to grasp the depth of your loss fully. There is ultimately no shortage of opportunities to feel the hollowness throughout your life, including what I experienced in my own life as a shift from a mellow, shy pre-teen girl to an often angry teenager, burdened with the weight of knowing her protectors were gone and yo-yo-ing between the resolve to be that to herself and hopelessness.

I appreciate that many of my guests and I are not experts, and plan to feature experts in the upcoming episodes and seasons of Granules of Grief with Refiloe. But from my experiences with grief and the experiences shared by all my guests so far, I think experts would agree that not developing and implementing healthy coping strategies only escalates to crisis level what could have been addressed at the onset. Even though loss is challenging, and many face barriers to access to support, it is best that we arm ourselves with grief awareness, both as grievers and supporters of grievers, including children.

If we don't [throw resources](https://winstonswish.org/) at it, it is grief that makes our life choices, if we make no move to master it. Master it not because we can ever be free of it, but because befriending grief is infinitely and unarguably our best bet.
